"H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5 (https://issforum.org/roundtables/PDF/Roundtable-XXIV-5.pdf)\nciteturn53view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 27; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://issforum.org/roundtables/PDF/Roundtable-XXIV-5.pdf\",\"lineno\":730}); Total lines: 983\nL646@P18: hint that Simon & Schuster were suggesting an autobiography.\nL647@P18: While I am pleased to have had this interesting archival source brought to my attention, McMeekin’s error is one of many\nL648@P18: such factual inaccuracies in the book.\nL649@P18: The most bizarre claim is that in spring 1943 Stalin approached Hitler with the offer of an armistice (453). But why do such\nL650@P18: a thing after the resounding Soviet victory at Stalingrad in January 1943? Why would Stalin risk alienating his British and\nL651@P18: American allies when, as McMeekin is at pains to point out, they were supplying him with massive material aid? During the\nL652@P18: war there were numerous fake news stories about peace feelers, many of them generated by intelligence agencies. The Soviets\nL653@P18: played this game, too, but despite decades of digging by historians, there is still no hard evidence of any serious intent on the\nL654@P18: part of either Stalin, Roosevelt, or Churchill to negotiate a separate peace with Hitler.58\nL655@P18: The book is extremely anti-Communist, and focuses on the dark side of Soviet behaviour during the war – the Katyn\nL656@P18: massacre of Polish POWs, the deportation of ethnic groups who were accused of collective disloyalty, and Stalin’s\nL657@P18: maltreatment of the families of Soviet POWs, including that of his son, Yakov, who died in German captivity in 1943. This\nL658@P18: is fair enough. While Stalin and the Red Army did, arguably, save the world from Hitler and the Nazis, the cost was brutal.\nL659@P18: In his conclusion McMeekin argues that the West should have confronted Stalin during the war and formed “a broad\nL660@P18: international coalition against totalitarian aggression,” an alliance that would have included “pro-Axis Hungary and Fascist\nL661@P18: Italy” (656). As to Hitler, he could have been dealt with by a peace deal that would have saved Western Europe from Nazi\nL662@P18: occupation and may even have extracted conquered Poland from his clutches (660). McMeekin’s speculation that standing\nL663@P18: up to Stalin would have turned out better than standing up to Hitler is as unconvincing as his book’s distortion of the\nL664@P18: complex and contradictory history of the Second World War.\nL665@P18: 56 On Faymonville: Mary E. Glantz, FDR and the Soviet Union (Lawrence: University Press of Kansas 2005) passim.\nL666@P18: 57 RGASP, F.558, Op.11, D.221, doc.19.\nL667@P18: 58 McMeekin’s source is a 1979 translation of a book about the Abwehr chief, Admiral Canaris, by the German journalist,\nL668@P18-19: Heinz Höhne.H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL669@P19: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL670@P19: 20 | Page\nL671@P19: Response by Sean McMeekin, Bard College\nL672@P19: I am grateful to H-Diplo for organizing this forum, which allows me to respond to several pointed critiques of my book\nL673@P19: Stalin’s War. I would also like to thank Warren Kimball for writing the introduction. I will begin with Mark Edele’s review,\nL674@P19: which is an updated and more heavily researched version of the one he first published in May 2021.\nL675@P19: It is not a careful review. Edele’s critique of alleged errors often rests on an absurdly literal-minded reading of the text, viz.,\nL676@P19: that I claim that Stalin deported all 200,000-odd Koreans on a single day in 1938, rather than against the backdrop of a Far\nL677@P19: Eastern War – something of course I do not say, and that cannot be inferred from my argument. Edele tries to refute my\nL678@P19: ‘claim’ that the British Cabinet was “grasping for legal straws to avoid entanglement with Stalin” by pointing out that there\nL679@P19: was a secret wiggle-out clause in the 25 August 1939 Mutual Assistance Pact with Poland. There was a secret clause – but the\nL680@P19: War Cabinet had not been informed, which is why Foreign Secretary Viscount Halifax gave the Cabinet the “odd legal\nL681@P19: briefing” I describe on page 111 of Stalin’s War. Here my book cites British War Cabinet minutes, accurately rendering a\nL682@P19: critical discussion word for word as it happened, along with a letter from France’s Ambassador to the French Foreign\nL683@P19: Minister from the Quai d’Orsay archives.59 Edele cites Wikipedia.\nL684@P19: Edeleclaims to have tried to track down my references, only for them to lead “nowhere,” for example “footnote” no. 2 on\nL685@P19: page 25 of Stalin’s War. There is no footnote on page 25. The endnote Edele refers to, which is found on page 676 of\nL686@P19: Stalin’s War, leads not “nowhere” but to Robert Tucker’s book Stalin in Power, where the citation is found and properly\nL687@P19: located in my endnote. To be certain, I pulled Tucker’s book down from the shelf, and the quote is indeed there, from “a\nL688@P19: report to the Central Committee of November 1928.”60 Edele claims that I have confused it with another speech from\nL689@P19: 1931, citing this latter text from marxists.org.\nL690@P19: These errors are typical of Edele’s review. Regarding Stalin’s alleged remarks of August 19, 1939, Edele writes that “even the\nL691@P19: article McMeekin cites for proof of the authenticity of this document notes that it seems to originate from an article\nL692@P19: published in the French La Revue universelle.” There are four factual errors in this sentence. First, the article I cite says\nL693@P19: nothing of the kind, concluding that the original version likely “leaked out from the Kremlin.”61 Nor do I cite this article as\nL694@P19: “proof,” noting quite openly in both the text of Stalin’s War (84) and in a footnote on that very pagethat the document’s\nL695@P19: authenticity is in question, and that many, though not all, historians view it as a forgery. Third, Edele says that the original is\nL696@P19: “in an archive holding foreign-origin documents,” sourcing this to the website of RGVA or the “Russian Government\nL697@P19: Archive of Military History,” containing records of the Soviet military and government organs between 1918-1940.62\nL698@P19: Finally, the source Edele cites to refute the supposed “proof” I do not claim to be offering is a Russian-language article\nL699@P19: published in Novy Mir by T.S. Bushuyeva in 1994. In fact Bushuyeva does write there that “it is necessary to compare [the\nL700@P19: 59 British war cabinet minutes, September 17, 1939, in The National Archives of the UK in Kew Gardens, CAB 65/1; and\nL701@P19: Corbin to Bonnet, September 20, 1939, in the Quai d’Orsay archives in La Courneuve, Paris, 92 CPCOM/286.\nL702@P19: 60 Robert C. Tucker, Stalin in Power. The Revolution from Above, 1928-1941 (New York: W.W. Norton, 1990), 70-71.\nL703@P19: 61 Carl O. Nordling, in Journal of Slavic Military Studies 19 (2006): 93-106.\nL704@P19: 62 Edele cites the boilerplate description of the ““Kharakteristika fondov” at http://rgvarchive.ru/ob-arkhive/kharakteristika\u0002fondov.shtml. This is the website for RGVA, the Russian State Government Military Archive. The principal holdings listed here are\nL705@P19: “documents on the history of the creation and development of the Soviet military authorities and the armed forces of the USSR (Red\nL706@P19: Army) in the period 1918-1940.” Edele has likely confused RGVA with TsGOA, formerly TsKhIDK, aka the “Osoby” or “Special\nL707@P19: Archive” of trophy documents from German-occupied Europe after World War II, which is located around the corner from RGVA (and\nL708@P19: now administered from the same central building). Having worked extensively in both archives, before and after the administrative\nL709@P19-20: reshuffle, I am aware of this difference. H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL710@P20: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL711@P20: 21 | Page\nL712@P20: French] version with the original,” but adds crucially, in a passage Edele ignores, “however, it is impossible to do this, since it\nL713@P20: is in the archive behind seven seals and in the near future they are unlikely to publish a facsimile of this undoubtedly\nL714@P20: historical document, which so openly exposed the aggressiveness of the USSR’s policy.”63\nL715@P20: Have I seen the original, hidden ‘behind seven seals,’ myself? No, nor do I claim to. In the darkening atmosphere of Russia\nL716@P20: today, it is unlikely any historian, let alone a western one, will ever again see the original. This does not mean it does not\nL717@P20: exist, or that a long and rich conversation around this document has not emerged among historians since 1994, a\nL718@P20: conversation which is, in itself, worthy of mention. If I am guilty of mentioning this source and discussing its provenance,\nL719@P20: then so are the other half-dozen-odd historians who have done the same.64\nL720@P20: In any case, one hardly has to rely on a single conversation to make the case that Soviet leader Joseph Stalin and Foreign\nL721@P20: Affairs Commissar Vyacheslav Molotov, by agreeing to a cynical deal with Germany’s Adolf Hitler, were angling to embroil\nL722@P20: Nazi Germany and the western powers into war. To imply that I have based my interpretation of Stalin’s foreign policy in\nL723@P20: 1939 on this one disputed source is absurd. I also cite Molotov’s draft for an agreement with Nazi Germany written in\nL724@P20: consultation with Stalin on the same day (August 19, 1939), in which, I say, “lies a critical clue to Stalin’s thinking,” along\nL725@P20: with the whole course of Soviet-German negotiations preceding the agreement signed on August 23. My interpretation of\nL726@P20: Stalin’s motives and intentions follows both the available information we have on his thinking and his decisions, and basic\nL727@P20: common sense: it is borne out, for example, by the contempt Stalin and Defense Commissar Kliment Voroshilov showed\nL728@P20: the British and French envoys to Moscow that August, by Stalin’s remarks to Giorgi Dimitrov in early September (the\nL729@P20: veracity of which neither Edele nor Geoffrey Roberts disputes), by Molotov’s frank admission to German Ambassador\nL730@P20: Count Friedrich-Werner von Schulenberg in September 1939 that the Soviets were hesitating before invading in order to\nL731@P20: avoid incurring British and French hostility, preferring that Britain and France fight Germany alone, by Molotov’s\nL732@P20: enthusiastic endorsement of the German invasion of France and the Low Countries the following spring (though hoping\nL733@P20: that the Germans would get more bloodied up than they did), etc.\nL734@P20: It is not clear whether Edele is arguing that Stalin was a principled devotee of “collective security” and the sanctity of east\nL735@P20: European borders who was prevented from carrying out such an altruistic policy only because of irrational western anti\u0002Communist prejudice, or that he was a pacifist seeking to stave off a European war, rather than exploit one. If not, his\nL736@P20: critique consists of mere hairsplitting, questioning the veracity of a single source among dozens – a source whose provenance\nL737@P20: I discuss carefully in the text, a footnote, and the source endnote. The critique also contains errors.\nL738@P20: As for Stalin’s speech to the academy graduates on May 5, 1941, Edele’s critique is incorrect. In the endnotes on pages 674-\nL739@P20: 75 of Stalin’s War, which Edele discusses, I cite no less than six different versions of the May 5 speech, including three\nL740@P20: 63 T. S. Bushuyeva, “Proklivaya – poprobuyu poniat’,” in Novyi Mir no. 12 (1994):\nL741@P20: https://magazines.gorky.media/novyi_mi/1994/12/proklinaya-poprobujte-ponyat.html. The passage in question reads as follows, in the\nL742@P20: original Russian: “Конечно, необходимо сравнить этот вариант с подлинником. Однако сделать это невозможно, так как он в\nL743@P20: архиве за семью печатями и в ближайшее время вряд ли станут обнародовать факсимиле этого безусловно исторического\nL744@P20: документа, столь откровенно обнажившего агрессивность политики СССР.”\nL745@P20: 64 For example, T. S. Bushuyeva, in the 1994 Novyi Mir article just cited; Carl O. Nordling, “Did Stalin Deliver His Alleged\nL746@P20: Speech of 19 August 1939?,” op cit; Marius Broekmeyer, in Stalin, the Russians, and their War, trans. Rosalind Buck (Madison: University\nL747@P20: of Wisconsin Press, 2004), 6-9; Oleg Khlevniuk, in Stalin. New Biography of a Dictator, trans. Nora Seligman (New Haven: Yale\nL748@P20: University Press, 2004), 167-168; Heinz Magenheimer, in Hitler’s War: Germany’s Military Strategy, 1940-1945 (Arms and Armour,\nL749@P20: 1999), p. 43 and accompanying endnote no. 79; and at much greater length by Mikhail Mel’tyukhov in Upushchennyi shans Stalina:\nL750@P20: Sovetskii soiuz i bor’ba za Evropu 1939-1941 (Moscow: Veche, 2000). This is by no means an exhaustive list, but it is broadly\nL751@P20: representative, including three Russians, two of whom (Bushuyeva and Mel’tyukhov) insist that there is a genuine Russian original, likely\nL752@P20: an unofficial transcript but one composed by someone present at a meeting with Stalin on this day, and one (Khlevniuk) who thinks the\nL753@P20-21: document a forgery. The three “western” authors all credit the source as plausibly genuine, while acknowledging the controversy – as I do. H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL754@P21: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL755@P21: 22 | Page\nL756@P21: Russian language transcripts written down, one by a government note-taker and two by eyewitnesses, while noting clearly\nL757@P21: whereand how they diverge. The most controversial bit, in which Stalin may or may not have fingered Germany as a likely\nL758@P21: military opponent, is clearly qualified in source endnote 7 on page 675, when I say that “after Stalin’s interruption of\nL759@P21: Khozin, eyewitness accounts diverge.” This citation comes not, pace Edele, from a “notorious German revisionist history”\nL760@P21: but from Stephen Kotkin’s carefully sourced 2018 Stalin biography.65\nL761@P21: As for Edele’s broader claim, that my whole account is derived from a “notorious German revisionist history,”66 Edele is\nL762@P21: presumably referring to the “Gehlen-Hilger” version of the speech, which has been dissected at length in the article by two\nL763@P21: professional military historians, Evan Mawdsley and Jürgen Förster (“Hitler and Stalin in Perspective: Secret Speeches on\nL764@P21: the Eve of Barbarossa”), which I cite.67 Mawdsley and Förster both credit this version as plausible and consistent with\nL765@P21: others, if far from definitive. What eyewitness account is ever definitive?\nL766@P21: Meanwhile, the whole dramatic scene I reproduce, wherein Stalin interrupted the speaker to correct him about the now\u0002outdated “peace” policy, is detailed in this “boring Soviet archival version” just as in the others I cite, with slightly different\nL767@P21: phrasing. The text is in fact both extremely detailed and broadly consistent with the other versions. Edele questions my use\nL768@P21: of this note-taker’s source on the basis that an archival file number does not match between the document volume I cite and\nL769@P21: an online version he cites from. I researched this volume in the Lenin Library, not online. I prefer doing my research in\nL770@P21: archives and libraries, rather than online.68\nL771@P21: Edele writes that he visited his “university’s library” to get to the bottom of my “convoluted endnote.” He finds the V. A.\nL772@P21: Nevezhin volume (Zastol’nye Rechi Stalina. Dokumentyi i materialyi) conforms to the “boring Soviet archival version.” The\nL773@P21: 65 Stephen Kotkin, Stalin, volume 2, Waiting for Hitler, 1928-1941 (New York: Penguin Press, 2018), 862.\nL774@P21: 66 Edele cites here two books, one of which, by Gustav Hilger, is nowhere mentioned in my book or bibliography. The other,\nL775@P21: which I cite exactly once in the entirety of Stalin’s War, is Ernst Topitsch’s Stalin’s War (London: Palgrave Macmillan, 1987) – a book I\nL776@P21: thought at least worthy of mention in a kind of hat tip after learning, long after I had chosen my own title, that someone else had beaten\nL777@P21: me to it. Topitsch’s book may or may not be “notorious,” but the author is Austrian, not German.\nL778@P21: 67 Evan Förster and Evan Mawdsley, “Hitler and Stalin in Perspective: Secret Speeches on the Eve of Barbarossa,” in War and\nL779@P21: History 11:1 (January 2004): 61-103.\nL780@P21: 68 “Vyistuplenie general’nogo sekretarya tsK VKp (b) i. V. stalina pered vyipusknikami voennyikh akademii RKKa v Kremle,”\nL781@P21: May 5, 1941, reproduced in the original Russian as no. 437 in 1941 God. Dokumenty, eds. Reshin et al. (1998), vol. 2, 158–59. The\nL782@P21: original of this document is sourced correctly by Reshin et al in the Stalin fond at the archive now known as RGASPI, fond 558, opis’ 1,\nL783@P21: del’ 3808, list’ 1–12, a full reference I give in tribute to them (Edele’s does not include the list or page numbers). It appears that, in this\nL784@P21: one instance, a typo has crept into my endnote no. 1 on page 674 such that the Stalin fond is listed as 538 instead of 558, which would be\nL785@P21: significant except that 1) having worked in it extensively in person, I cite the Stalin fond no. 558 from RGASPI correctly several hundred\nL786@P21: other times in the endnotes, and in the bibliography, showing that this is an idle typo; and 2) in this case I am not even sourcing a text, but\nL787@P21: crediting the editors of a document volume for having located and reproduced a source which is no longer being given out regularly. If any\nL788@P21: intrepid readers try to track down the original document at RGASPI, they will of course be told that the Stalin fond is no. 558, as is\nL789@P21: abundantly clear in the hundreds of other references to it in my book. Readers can also find the source in the volume I cite, which is the\nL790@P21: point of a source note.\nL791@P21: Moreover, to return to the substance of the argument, even this “boring” version by the Soviet government note-taker has\nL792@P21: Stalin forcibly interrupting Khozin when he gives his toast to Stalin’s “peace policy” (tost za mirnuyu Stalinskuyu vneshnuyu politiku) to\nL793@P21: correct him, emphasizing the need, now that the army has been thoroughly revamped and modernized, etc., to “shift from a defensive to\nL794@P21: an offensive posture,” (teper’ nado pereiti ot oboronyi k nastupleniyu), “to a military policy of offensive actions” (ot oboronyi pereiti k voennoi\nL795@P21: k voennoi politike nastupatel'nyikh deistvii), to “re-organize our training, our propaganda, our agitation, our press in an offensive spirit” (ot\nL796@P21-22: oboronyi pereiti k voennoi k voennoi politike nastupatel'nyikh deistvii), etc. I find nothing “boring” about this account. H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL797@P22: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL798@P22: 23 | Page\nL799@P22: Nevezhin volume reproduces 5 May 1941 diary entries for V. A. Malyshev and G. M. Dimitrov, both of whom were present\nL800@P22: for Stalin’s speech and noted the interruption and the sharp change in doctrine. (They did not, of course, record the entire\nL801@P22: speech – why would they have? These are diary entries. They noted the important and novel part of the evening’s events,\nL802@P22: i.e., Stalin’s interruption.) Malyshev (279-80) has Stalin interrupting to say that the Red Army must now educate its\nL803@P22: commanders “in the spirit of the offensive” (Vospityivat’ ikh v dukhe nastupleniya). Dimitrov’s diary entry (281) repeats a\nL804@P22: similar phrase, then has Stalin conclude more dramatically that the Red Army “must prepare for war” (nado gotovit’sya k\nL805@P22: voine).69\nL806@P22: Somehow, despite the “research” Edele claims to have done digging into these source notes from what is, after all, a brief four\nL807@P22: page narrative prologue, he has missed all these citations. Nor does he refer to my citation of an original transcript of\nL808@P22: another debriefing of a Soviet officer present at the speech (not the “Gehlen/Hilger” version), which I discovered myself, a\nL809@P22: document which is now freely available to researchers at the Politiches Archiv des Auswärtigen Amtes (PAAA) in Berlin.70\nL810@P22: Edele asserts that the only reliable account of this episode is the “boring” version of a Soviet government note-taker – which\nL811@P22: turns out to be far from boring if you read it. My approach was to seek to disentangle truth from whitewashed official\nL812@P22: narratives and breathe life into history by citing also from diary entries, witness debriefings, and memoirs – while noting, as I\nL813@P22: very carefully do, where accounts of a contested event diverge.\nL814@P22: Edele writes that I make the “claim that this book is based on revelations from the Soviet archives. It is not.” What I write in\nL815@P22: the book’s introduction on page 6 is that I have “re-examined the conflict as a whole in light of newly available Russian\nL816@P22: documents,” including “brand new” discoveries of my own (for example, the Soviet Ministry of Aerodrome Construction\nL817@P22: files for 1941, the Politburo Special Files for June 1941, real-time telegraphic exchanges between Molotov and Stalin during\nL818@P22: the Berlin summit of November 1940, data on how Lend-Lease tanks, trucks, and warplanes were incorporated into Red\nL819@P22: Army units) along with those in document volumes edited by Russian researchers whom I cite copiously and thank\nL820@P22: profusely. I also draw heavily on US, British, French, German, Polish, and Bulgarian archival sources, many of which,\nL821@P22: particularly those from Bulgaria and Poland, have not before been used by western historians. Now that Russian archives\nL822@P22: may be off limits to foreigners for the foreseeable future, this is the only way to research Soviet history.\nL823@P22: Roberts disagrees with my interpretation of Stalin’s foreign policy and motives. He writes that, masses of plain-as-day\nL824@P22: evidence to the contrary, “Stalin was far from relishing the prospect of a new imperialist world war,” that Stalin sincerely\nL825@P22: believed in “collective security” and had been “striving for…an anti-Hitler coalition with Britain and France…since 1933.”\nL826@P22: The historical evidence, however, shows that this interpretation of Stalin’s foreign policy is groundless. As I explain in the\nL827@P22: book, the partition of Poland was a Soviet proposal, not a German one, first mooted in Soviet theoretical journals in 1938\nL828@P22: and then pursued vigorously through back channels until coming to fruition in August 1939. The evidence I cite to explain\nL829@P22: Stalin’s disinterest in “collective security” also includes Stalin’s demotion of Maxim Litvinov from the European desk in May\nL830@P22: 1937, his “chestnuts” (of, as Roberts prefers, “fire-raking with someone else’s hands”) speech in March 1939, his sacking of\nL831@P22: Litvinov and the purging of the Soviet Foreign Ministry of Jews in early May 1939, and the Soviet approaches to the\nL832@P22: Germans which preceded the Molotov-Ribbentrop Pact. I also point out that the USSR did not share a border with\nL833@P22: Czechoslovakia, rendering any putative Soviet guarantee of that country’s borders problematic at best, and the unambiguous\nL834@P22: rejection by Ambassador Ivan Maisky of Prime Minister Neville Chamberlain’s March 1939 guarantee to Poland – a\nL835@P22: rejection repeated publicly by a Soviet TASS communiqué – which made crystal clear Stalin’s real views on ‘collective\nL836@P22: security.’\nL837@P22: 69 Nevezhin, V.A., ed., Zastol’nyie rechi Stalina. Dokumentyi i materialyi (Moscow: Airo-XX, 2003).\nL838@P22: 70 “Übersetzung des Berichtes des Generalmajors Naumow über ein Bankett in Moskau am 5.5.41 anläßlich des Abschlusses\nL839@P22-23: eines Kriegsakademie-Lehrganges,” in PAAA, R 104585.H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL840@P23: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL841@P23: 24 | Page\nL842@P23: It is not I, but Roberts in his review, who bases his interpretation of Stalin’s foreign policy on a single source – a few remarks\nL843@P23: of regret Stalin expressed to Dimitrov in early September 1939 that, in a world free of anti-Communist prejudice, he might\nL844@P23: have done a deal with France and Britain instead of with Nazi Germany (“we preferred agreements with the so-called\nL845@P23: democratic countries and therefore conducted negotiations. But the English and French wanted us for farmhands and at no\nL846@P23: cost.”) My interpretation of Stalin’s foreign policy fits the known facts of history as it happened; Roberts’ rests on a\nL847@P23: counterfactual argument.\nL848@P23: As Ian Johnson points out in his deeply researched recent study, Faustian Bargain. The Soviet-German Partnership and the\nL849@P23: Origins of the Second World War (Oxford, 2021), which came out just after my book did, the partnership between Hitler\nL850@P23: and Stalin hardly came out of the blue on August 23, 1939.71 It was not only the prospect of carving up Poland and regaining\nL851@P23: former Russian territory in Finland, the Baltic states, and Romania which tempted Stalin and Molotov to the negotiating\nL852@P23: table. As early as January 28, 1939 – long before the “chestnuts” speech, before Stalin sacked Litvinov as an olive branch to\nL853@P23: Hitler, Soviet officials Klim Voroshilov and Anastas Mikoyan presented the German government with a military\u0002technological wish list seventeen pages long, listing 112 items of key need for Soviet military modernization, including “four\nL854@P23: complete fighter and bomber prototypes, seven engine designs, thirteen different machine gun and bomb designs, nine types\nL855@P23: of laboratory equipment, and ten kinds of optical and electrical equipment.” As Johnson concludes, in every possible sense,\nL856@P23: from territorial gains to critical technology transfer, “the Germans had far more to offer than the British and French.”72\nL857@P23: As for the Raymond Faymonville case, Roberts accuses me of making unsourced assertions (“McMeekin does not cite any\nL858@P23: source for these contentions”). In fact endnote 19 on 723-24 of Stalin’s War, clearly indicates that my discussion of\nL859@P23: Faymonville was not based on The Venona Secrets, which he surmises was my “probable source,” but on the papers of\nL860@P23: Colonel Ivan D. Yeaton, who spent much of his career in army intelligence tracking Faymonville.73 The Venona Secrets has\nL861@P23: little to say about the Faymonville case beyond the “honey trap” story which Roberts mentions. The point of my discussion\nL862@P23: of Faymonville in Stalin’s War is not to gossip about his alleged seduction, but to discuss what he did to influence US policy\nL863@P23: vis-à-vis Russia. Roberts also refers incorrectly to “General Philip Faymonville”; Faymonville’s rank at the time was Colonel.\nL864@P23: This error seemingly stems from the Venona Secrets, where Faymonville is described on p. 218 as “the general.”74\nL865@P23: As for the substantial point at issue, I am not sure what to make of Roberts’ curious argument that Faymonville was, if not a\nL866@P23: Soviet spy, then an “an American intelligence asset” with whom “an NKVD agent called ‘Electric’ was tasked with getting\nL867@P23: close to,” though while allowing that this might be “a cover story to disguise the fact that Faymonville was indeed a Soviet\nL868@P23: agent” and noting that, in any case, “Faymonville was highly sympathetic to the Soviets and their war effort” – just as I say he\nL869@P23: was. There are plenty of works published in English which still huffily deny that Faymonville had any connection with\nL870@P23: Soviet intelligence, such as Susan Butler’s 2015 study Roosevelt and Stalin: Portrait of a Partnership.\nL871@P23: 75 It seems that Roberts\nL872@P23: and I agree on this point.\nL873@P23: 71 Ian Johnson, Faustian Bargain. The Soviet-German Partnership and the Origins of the Second World War (New York:\nL874@P23: Oxford University Press, 2021).\nL875@P23: 72 Johnson, Faustian Bargain, 202, 207.\nL876@P23: 73 Hoover Institution Archives, Collection: Yeaton, Ivan D., box 1 (Correspondence, 1939-41) and unpublished memoirs.\nL877@P23: 74 Herbert Romerstein and Eric Breindel, The Venona Secrets. Exposing Soviet Espionage and America’s Traitors (Washington,\nL878@P23: D.C.: Regnery, 2000/2001), 218.\nL879@P23-24: 75 Susan Butler, Roosevelt and Stalin: Portrait of a Partnership (New York: Knopf, 2015).H-Diplo Roundtable XXIV-5\nL880@P24: © 2022 The Authors | CC BY-NC-ND 3.0 US\nL881@P24: 25 | Page\nL882@P24: Likewise, I am unsure what to make of Roberts’ gratitude for my directing him to a book offer to Stalin made by Simon &\nL883@P24: Schuster in 1943. The original letter, written by Lincoln Schuster himself, discusses, as a model, the Joseph Davies memoir\u0002Stalinist-advocacy vehicle Mission to Moscow published by S & S and invites Stalin to “give the world a far more\nL884@P24: comprehensive statement of Soviet war and peace aims” than was offered in newspaper interviews, this time “in book\nL885@P24: form.”76 Obviously, Stalin would not have been expected to actually writethe book, but the aim was clearly to interview him\nL886@P24: and put together a flattering quickie-autobiographical portrait of Stalin, his background, his views, how he saw the world,\nL887@P24: etc.\nL888@P24: I am glad that Vojin Majstorovic’s review discusses some of the book’s main themes. I do not in fact argue in the book that a\nL889@P24: “German victory was preferable in the war,” nor does Majstorovic cite any text from my book suggesting this.\nL890@P24: I do argue that Prime Minister Winston Churchill’s lobbying for a secondary Balkan operation at Teheran was more\n"
